From 096ad7ed942aa5e72394d9f783ede7fb40c610b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 01:35:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] NHFLOW: destructors free what the constructors allocate
 (no behaviour change)

The NHFLOW interfaces had no virtual destructor (sixdof, nhflow_pressure,
nhflow_reconstruct, nhflow_convection, nhflow_fsf, nhflow_diffusion,
nhflow_turbulence, vrans_nhflow, density, nhflow_flux_build, wind), so a
delete through the interface pointer skipped the derived destructor, and
the derived destructors were empty. Level 0 never deletes these objects;
the mesh refinement does, every time a regrid replaces a patch (22 MB lost
over four layout changes of a 2 x 1 m test tank, valgrind).

- virtual destructors on the interfaces
- the destructors free their arrays and owned objects: nhflow_momentum_RK2/
  RK3 (stage and diffusion arrays, wind), nhflow_HLL/HLLC (flux builder),
  nhflow_reconstruct_hires/weno (DFDX), nhflow_pjm/pjm_corr/pjm_hs (density,
  Poisson, P0/PCORR), nhflow_forcing (FX, FY, FZ), nhflow_geometry (ray-cast
  and reinitialisation arrays, set to nullptr when not allocated),
  nhflow_fsf_f (wet-dry work array)
- lexer::gridini sets up the bed list of the solid forcing (gcsldfbed4) as
  the eta list; its count was uninitialised and ghostcell::solid_forcing_bed
  looped over it (valgrind: conditional jump on uninitialised value)

Output byte-identical to ae7ea716f: CFD regression quick set and 6DOF
cases, NHFLOW floating box (1 and 2 ranks), NHFLOW AMR flume, FNPF 6DOF
AMR decay.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014HX78HZiTjrxAkgQpNoML1
